From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: glacier <hyouga.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 20:44:46 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Bucket the chunk entity slices by X

An entity slice holds every entity in a 16x16x16 section, and the queries that
dominate the server thread ask it for the few entities inside a box barely larger
than one entity. Counted on a crowded world: 87 candidates scanned per call and
1.3 of them matching, so 98.5% of the scan was thrown away.

Each slice now also carries 16 buckets, one per block column in X, and a query
walks only the buckets its box can reach. An entity is bucketed by its minimum X
corner, so the range starts one pad below the box - the pad being at least the
width of the widest entity the slice has held - which is what makes the bucketed
answer identical to the flat one rather than merely close.

The index is exact, not refreshed once a tick: setEntityBoundingBox is the single
point where a bounding box changes and it updates the bucket from there, so there
is no window in which an entity sits in a bucket that no longer matches where it
is. A once-a-tick index would have been fine at chunk granularity and wrong at one
block: a knockback or a teleport would leave an entity unfindable for a tick, and
that is an arrow that does not hit.

entitySlices stays authoritative. The buckets are derived: every path that is not
a positional query still reads the slice, a slice below the threshold has no
bucket array and is walked flat, and debugVerifyAthenaIndexes now checks the
buckets against the slice they came from on every tick.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

