+    // Athena start - a one-block-wide X bucket index over each entity slice.
+    //
+    // A slice holds every entity in a 16x16x16 section, and the queries that dominate the server
+    // thread ask it for the few entities inside a box barely larger than one entity. Counted on a
+    // crowded world: 87 candidates scanned per call and 1.3 of them matching, so 98.5% of the scan
+    // is thrown away. Bucketing the slice by the block column of each entity's minimum X corner
+    // lets a query touch only the buckets its box can reach.
+    //
+    // entitySlices stays authoritative. The buckets are a derived index: everything that is not a
+    // positional query still reads the slice, and a slice with no bucket array is walked flat, so
+    // a missing index costs correctness nothing. Small slices never get one - below the threshold
+    // building and maintaining it costs more than the scan it saves.
+    //
+    // The index is kept exact rather than refreshed once a tick. Entity.setEntityBoundingBox is
+    // the one place a bounding box changes, and it calls updateEntityBucket from there, so a
+    // bucketed query returns exactly the entities the flat walk would have returned - there is no
+    // window in which an entity sits in a bucket that no longer matches where it is.
+    private static final int BUCKET_THRESHOLD = 24;
+    private final List<Entity>[][] entityBuckets = (List<Entity>[][]) new List[16][];
+    private final int[] bucketPad = new int[16];
+    // Athena end
